  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS simulator UDID or Android emulator serial, or Mac app id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * Text to put on the device clipboard and paste into the focused field. Supports `{{secret:<NAME>}}` placeholders, resolved on the tool-server from the `ARGENT_SECRET_<NAME>` environment variable or an argent secrets file (the same sources as `keyboard`), so a credential never enters your context and is never echoed back. If the secret is not set, the failure lists the available names and every source it looked in; ask the user to add it there, NEVER ask for the value in the conversation.
   * @minLength 1
   */
  text: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent.describe","description":"Get the accessibility / DOM element tree for the current screen.
Use when you need element frames or labels and have no fresh tree: most interaction tools (not tv-remote or shake) already return one after each action, or name an identical earlier one by its tree id.
On iOS it reads the AXRuntime accessibility service, so it sees whatever is visible, including system dialogs and permission prompts. While a system dialog is visible it returns the dialog's elements (buttons, text); otherwise the foreground app's. On a physical iOS device it covers only the app started with launch-app; to read system dialogs there, launch-app `com.apple.springboard` first.
On Android it reads the tree through Argent's UiAutomation helper, falling back to `uiautomator dump`. On Chromium it walks the DOM over the Chrome DevTools Protocol: every visible element with its ARIA role, accessible name and bounding rect.
On Vega (Fire TV) it reads the on-device automation toolkit; `[focused]`/`[selected]` mark the D-pad cursor, which you move with `tv-remote` (Vega has no touch).
On a Mac app it reads the app's accessibility tree with web view content and React Native testIDs merged in; frames are fractions of the app window, grown to include sheets and menus past it.
On a TV target (Apple TV / Android TV: a `list-devices` entry with runtimeKind 'tv') it returns the FOCUSED element and the FOCUSABLE elements instead, since a TV UI has no tap coordinates. Move the highlight with `tv-remote`, then call describe again to confirm where focus landed.

Returns `{ description, source }`: `description` renders the tree one line per element, with its role, label/value/id, VoiceOver hint (iOS simulator), interactivity flags and frame. Frames are normalized [0,1] fractions of the screen / window width and height (not pixels); tap an element at the centre of its frame. The tree has no z-order or occlusion information: an element may be covered by an overlay (e.g. a toolbar over list rows), so check a screenshot when a tap lands unexpectedly.
Fails if the tree cannot be read, e.g. on a physical iOS device before launch-app has started an app.

For UIKit properties of a view (class name, accessibilityIdentifier), use native-find-views (iOS simulator only).
For React Native component names, or a testID describe does not list, use debugger-component-tree.","signature":"tools.argent.describe({
  /**
   * Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Vega serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id).
   * @minLength 1
   */
  udid: string,
  /**
   * App bundle ID, used as a target hint on iOS when the AX-service returns no elements and describe falls back to native-devtools inspection. If omitted, the fallback auto-detects the frontmost connected app. Ignored on every other platform, including a physical iOS device.
   */
  bundleId?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]","description":"Press the left mouse button at a start point, move to an end point, and release: a desktop mouse drag in a Chromium app or a Mac app, with mouse moves interpolated at ~60fps. All positions are fractions of the window width/height (not pixels), the same space as gesture-tap and describe, except that on Chromium exactly 1.0 lands one pixel inside the window edge.
Use for slider thumbs, drag-and-drop, text selection, or draggable UI elements. Dragging never scrolls content on desktop; use gesture-scroll for lists/pages. Chromium and Mac apps only: on iOS/Android use gesture-swipe. On a Mac app the same start and end point is a long press, and a drop lands only inside the app's own windows.
Returns { dragged: true, timestampMs }. Fails if the Chromium CDP session is not reachable for the given device.","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-drag"]({
  /** Target Chromium device id from `list-devices` (chromium-cdp-<port>), or a Mac app id. */
  udid: string,
  /** Press x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromX: number,
  /** Press y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  fromY: number,
  /** Release x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toX: number,
  /** Release y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  toY: number,
  /**
   * Total drag duration in milliseconds (default 300, 800 for a long press on a Mac app); the button stays down this long. Maximum: 10000.
   * @maximum 10000
   */
  durationMs?: number,
  /**
   * Whether the drag releases with momentum; default true (a constant-speed drag). false decelerates into the release point, so an app that derives a fling from the release velocity (carousels, drag libraries) applies little to no momentum: use it when the drag must stop where it was aimed. Under ~100ms durationMs some fling survives; keep the default 300 when the fling must be fully suppressed.
   */
  momentum?: boolean,
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]","description":"Press the device screen (iOS simulator or physical device, Android emulator, Mac app, or Chromium app) at a point given as fractions of the screen width and height (not pixels): a Down event followed by an Up event at the same spot. On a Mac app it is a click, and x/y are fractions of the area describe reports, not of the window.
Use when tapping a button, link, or any tappable element on the screen.
Returns { tapped: true, timestampMs }. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the device backend (simulator-server / emulator / Chromium CDP) is unreachable.
Before tapping, take the coordinates from `describe` on every platform (on Chromium it walks the DOM). More information in `argent-device-interact` skill","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-tap"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID, Android serial, Mac app id, or Chromium id). */
  udid: string,
  /** Horizontal position: 0 = left edge, 1 = right edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  x: number,
  /** Vertical position: 0 = top edge, 1 = bottom edge. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
  y: number,
  /**
   * Number of taps/clicks dispatched as ONE gesture inside the OS double-tap window (2 = double-tap / double-click), which two separate tap calls cannot guarantee. On physical iOS, higher counts loop single taps, which a double-tap recognizer may pair. Default 1. Range: 1 to 10.
   * @integer @minimum 1 @maximum 10
   */
  clickCount?: number,
  /**
   * Mouse button, Mac app only: "right" is a right-click, for example to open a context menu; any other device refuses it. Default "left".
   */
  button?: "left" | "right",
}): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["list-devices"]","description":"List iOS simulators, Android emulators, connected physical Android devices, running Chromium apps, and Vega (Fire TV) devices in one place.
Call this at the start of a session to pick a target id ('udid' for iOS entries, 'serial' for Android/Vega entries, 'id' for Chromium) to pass to interaction tools, and to see which targets are already running.
Returns { devices, avds } where each device carries a 'platform' discriminator ('ios', 'android', 'chromium', or 'vega'); 'avds' lists Android AVDs bootable via boot-device. A Vega VVD is listed under 'devices' whether running or stopped (state 'running'/'stopped'); start a stopped one with boot-device using its 'vvdImage'.
Android entries also carry a 'kind' ('emulator' for a local AVD, 'device' for a physical phone on USB / wireless adb). Physical phones use the same interaction tools as emulators and need no boot-device (just connect the phone with USB debugging authorised).
Physical iPhones appear as iOS entries with kind 'device' (no iPads); no boot-device. State 'connected' = cabled and usable; 'paired' = not reachable over USB, never auto-bound.
TV targets are tagged with runtimeKind 'tv' (Apple TV simulators, Android TV / leanback devices). They are focus-driven, not touch-driven: use `describe` to read focus, `tv-remote` for remote presses, and `keyboard` to type, rather than the coordinate/gesture tools.
iOS simulators from an additional CoreSimulator device set (the 'ios.additionalDeviceSets' configuration, e.g. devices created by Radon IDE) are tagged with their 'deviceSet' path; the same tools drive them by udid, but they run headless (no Simulator.app window).
A foldable iOS simulator (the iPhone Duo) or Android emulator (and its AVD) has 'foldable: true'; use the `fold` tool to fold or unfold it.
Chromium apps are discovered by probing CDP debugging ports (default 9222; extend via the ARGENT_CHROMIUM_PORTS=<comma-separated-ports> env var). They must already be running with --remote-debugging-port=<port>; use boot-device with electronAppPath to launch one.
Mac apps that argent started (boot-device with macosAppPath) are listed while they run, as platform 'macos' with the 'id' macos-app-<bundle id>.
Booted/ready devices are listed first, but use a physical phone (kind 'device') only when the user asks for one. A platform is silently omitted when its CLI is not found; an empty result usually means xcode-select, Android platform-tools, or the Vega SDK is not installed.","signature":"tools.argent["list-devices"](): Promise<unknown>"},{"path":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]","description":"Send a sequence of touch events for complex gestures.
Use for: long press, drag-and-drop, custom scroll, pinch (second touch point).
For simple taps use the gesture-tap tool. For straight-line scrolling use the gesture-swipe tool. Not supported on a Mac app: use gesture-tap, gesture-drag and gesture-scroll there.
For pinch gestures use gesture-pinch. For rotation gestures use gesture-rotate.
All x/y values are fractions of the screen width/height (not pixels).
Physical iOS: a Down, any Moves, then an Up; x2/y2 adds a second finger. A plain Down then Up: same point = press-hold, other point = drag (Up delayMs = rest before the move); one Move at the Down point between them = long-press pickup (Move delayMs = hold, Up delayMs = move time). Any other train plays as timed paths, each event waiting its delayMs, and needs the app drawn in portrait.
Returns { events: number } with the total count of events dispatched. On physical iOS, reactivated: true = app was re-fronted; re-describe. Fails if the target device is not booted or an event type is invalid.

Example long-press at center:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.5,"delayMs":800}]

Example pick up a list item with a long press, then drag it up:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6,"delayMs":800},{"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3,"delayMs":500}]

Example smooth scroll down:
  [{"type":"Down","x":0.5,"y":0.7},
   {"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.6},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.5},{"type":"Move","x":0.5,"y":0.4},
   {"type":"Up","x":0.5,"y":0.3}]

Example pinch-to-zoom (with interpolate:10 for smoothness):
  events: [{"type":"Down","x":0.4,"y":0.5,"x2":0.6,"y2":0.5},
           {"type":"Up","x":0.2,"y":0.5,"x2":0.8,"y2":0.5}]
  interpolate: 10","signature":"tools.argent["gesture-custom"]({
  /** Target device id from `list-devices` (iOS UDID or Android serial). */
  udid: string,
  /** Touch events, dispatched in order. */
  events: Array<{
      /** Touch event type */
      type: "Down" | "Move" | "Up",
      /** Touch x. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x: number,
      /** Touch y. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y: number,
      /** Second finger x, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      x2?: number,
      /** Second finger y, for two-finger gestures. Range: 0 to 1. @minimum 0 @maximum 1 */
      y2?: number,
      /** Delay before this event in milliseconds (default 16ms ≈ 60fps) */
      delayMs?: number,
    }>,
  /**
   * Number of Move events to insert between each pair of consecutive events, linearly interpolating both x,y and x2,y2 and splitting the delay evenly, for a smooth gesture. Default: no interpolation.
   */
  interpolate?: number,
}): Promise<unknown>"}],"remaining":60,"next":{"offset":10}}

